  "doc.title": "How a Pile of Switches Learned to Do Arithmetic",
  "meta.desc": "A computer is built from just one kind of part: a switch that is either on or off. Starting from that switch, we build logic gates, an adder, memory, a clock, and a tiny machine that multiplies by following a program. Each step uses only what was built in the step before.",
  "nav.back": "← Knowledge index",
