  "hero.kicker": "Computing and intelligence · a long essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "How a Pile of Switches Learned to Do Arithmetic",
  "hero.sub": "From a single switch to a machine that multiplies by following a program,<br>without adding a single new part along the way",
  "hero.lead": "Take a computer apart and keep going down until only one kind of part is left: a switch that is either conducting or not. A phone chip today holds more than ten billion of them (Apple's A14 from 2020 has about 11.8 billion), and the first commercial microprocessor, Intel's 4004 of 1971, had about 2,300. Nothing else is there. There is no special part that “understands” math.",
  "hero.question": "So the question is simple: <strong>using nothing but on and off, how can anything add numbers, remember things, or work through a list of instructions one step at a time?</strong> The eleven chapters below are one continuous build. Each chapter's parts are made only from things the reader has already put together, and later chapters keep pulling earlier parts back out and reusing them.",
  "hero.lampsCapStill": "The row of lamps above is the counter we build in Chapter 7. Your system has reduced motion turned on, so it is holding still here.",
  "hero.lampsCap": "The row of lamps above is counting. It is the counter we build in Chapter 7, running gate by gate in a simulation right now.",
  "hero.note": "This page was written by an AI. From Chapter 2 on, circuits are simulated gate by gate, starting from the NAND gate, by code on the page; the numbers come from the simulation, not from a lookup table. There are two exceptions, each flagged where it happens: the machine in Chapter 9 models its memory, registers and instruction decoder by behavior only, and Chapter 10 computes floating-point results with exact integer arithmetic instead of gates. The other simplifications: most gates are given the same delay, and voltages are only 0 or 1. The end of each chapter says what was simplified.",

  "toc.title": "Contents",

  "ch1.title": "Switches Control Switches",
  "ch1.law": "The output of one switch can press another switch. Two switches in series give AND, two in parallel give OR, and a switch fitted the other way round (normally closed) gives NOT.",
  "ch1.p1": "A bank vault often needs two people, each turning their own key, before the door opens. The two locks are <strong>in series</strong> on one line: if either key is missing, nothing gets through. A car's dome light works the other way: it turns on when any door opens. Several door switches are <strong>in parallel</strong>, and whichever one connects is enough.",
  "ch1.p2": "The key step is letting a switch be pressed by electricity instead of a hand. A relay works like this: when current flows through its coil, an electromagnet pulls the contacts together. So the output of one circuit (whether a wire is live) can directly press the next switch. There are two ways to wire a switch. A <strong>normally open</strong> switch is off until pressed; a <strong>normally closed</strong> switch is on until pressed, and then it turns off. Today's chips use transistors in place of relays, with no moving contacts, but they do the same job.",
  "ch1.model.title": "Two switches and a lamp",
  "ch1.model.how": "First choose series or parallel, then decide whether each switch is normally open or normally closed. Press A and press B, and try all four combinations. The table on the right records each row you have tried.",
