  "gate.desc.AND": "The lamp is on only when both are pressed. That is the bank vault's two keys.",
  "gate.desc.OR": "The lamp is on when either one is pressed. That is the car's dome light.",
  "gate.desc.NAND": "The lamp is off only when both are pressed, and on at all other times. It is the opposite of AND. You will need it in the next chapter, so remember this wiring: two normally closed switches in parallel.",
  "gate.desc.NOR": "The lamp is on only when neither is pressed. It is the opposite of OR.",
  "gate.desc.A_NOT_B": "The lamp is on only when A is pressed and B is not.",
  "gate.desc.B_NOT_A": "The lamp is on only when B is pressed and A is not.",
  "gate.desc.A_OR_NOT_B": "The lamp is off only when B is pressed and A is not.",
  "gate.desc.B_OR_NOT_A": "The lamp is off only when A is pressed and B is not.",
  "ch1.p3": "What you just did was write out the whole behavior of a circuit as a <strong>truth table</strong>: each of the two inputs is either pressed or not, which makes four rows, and each row records an output. A small circuit that can produce a table like this is called a <strong>logic gate</strong>. With just one normally closed switch, pressing it turns the lamp off and releasing it turns the lamp on. That is a <strong>NOT gate</strong>, which flips its input. In 1937 Claude Shannon, in his master's thesis at MIT, showed that relay circuits like these are the same thing as Boolean algebra (an algebra with only two values, true and false), so circuits could be designed with algebra.",
  "ch1.limit": "In the model, “power on” and “power off” are clean 0 and 1. Real wires carry continuous voltages. A circuit reads anything above one threshold as 1 and anything below another as 0, and the signal has to cross the gap between them quickly. Chapter 6 runs into a case where it cannot cross.",

  "ch2.title": "One Gate Is Enough",
  "ch2.law": "With NAND gates alone, you can build every other gate. From here on, the whole machine uses only this one kind of part.",
  "ch2.p1": "If a set of building bricks came in only one shape, you could still build a castle or a spaceship from it, just using more bricks. Logic gates have a brick like this: it is the circuit from the last chapter with two normally closed switches in parallel. It is the <strong>NAND gate</strong>. Its output is 0 when both inputs are 1, and 1 otherwise.",
  "ch2.p2": "Now you will build the other gates yourself, using only NAND gates. Each gate has two inputs, which you can connect to A, to B, or to the output of an earlier gate. The last gate you connect is the output of the whole circuit.",
  "ch2.model.title": "NAND gates only",
  "ch2.model.how": "Choose a target, then pick the wiring for each gate's two inputs. The table below compares your circuit with the target one row at a time. Press A and B to watch signals travel between the gates. If you cannot find a solution, you can look at the answer.",
